Task: Extension manifest and menu entry stub (Phase 1, Track foundation-core_20260918)

What was done:
- Wrote the manifest declaring the extension and pointing at a single entry
  point script.
- The entry point creates one menu group under the application's script menu
  and registers one command inside it.
- Installed it into the application's user extension directory and verified the
  load in the application's own log.
- Verified registration positively rather than by absence of errors: a batch
  run called the registered command successfully and a deliberately invented
  command name failed, which also proves the extension's startup function runs
  in batch mode, not only when the interface is up.

Findings worth keeping:
- A symbolic link into the extension directory is silently ignored: the scan
  enumerates real directories only. Development therefore needs a copy step,
  now documented in the process document.
- The application's log is truncated on every run and only written in verbose
  mode, so checking it requires the verbose flag on that same run.
- The application's version value is an object, not a string, and must be
  converted before being concatenated.
- Showing a version number inside the About box was dropped: reading the
  manifest back at runtime needs file access, which triggers the permission
  prompt this project deliberately avoids, and hardcoding it would duplicate a
  value the manifest already owns.

Files changed:
- package.json, main.lua, conductor/workflow.md

Why:
Everything later in this track needs a proven path from an installed extension
to a running command; without it, a failing command could not be distinguished
from a failing installation.
